const QUIZ=[
/* ---------------- OT vs GT vs E-com ---------------- */
 {t:'ot',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',trap:true,
  q:'The whole GT–OT difference can be compressed into one line. Which is it?',
  opts:['GT is relationship-led and outlet-led; OT is system-led and category-led',
        'GT is system-led and category-led; OT is relationship-led and outlet-led',
        'GT is price-led and scheme-led; OT is brand-led and display-led today',
        'GT is distributor-led and rural; OT is retailer-led and mostly urban'],
  why:'<b>GT = relationship + outlet. OT = system + category.</b> A kirana sale is won one outlet at a time, through the salesman’s relationship with the owner. A DMart sale is won through systems (central buying, DC replenishment, POS data) and is managed category by category.<div class="work">GT  → relationship-led · outlet-led\nOT  → system-led · category-led</div>The trap is the swapped version — it reads fine if you skim. Remember it by the kirana: no planogram, no category manager, just the owner and the salesman.<br><br>This one line explains nearly every other row in the comparison tables.'},

 {t:'ot',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'In Organised Trade, who is the <b>decision maker</b> an FMCG company has to convince?',
  opts:['The category manager / buyer / commercial team of the chain',
        'The individual store owner, who decides store by store',
        'The platform category manager plus the ranking algorithm',
        'The distributor’s salesman, who places stock at each outlet'],
  why:'<b>OT decisions sit with the chain’s category manager, buyer or commercial team</b> — centrally, not in the store.<div class="work">OT     → category manager / buyer / commercial team\nGT     → individual retailer\nE-com  → platform category manager + algorithm/customer</div>“Store owner” is the GT answer and “algorithm” is the e-commerce answer. The salesman influences GT, but he is not the decision maker anywhere.<br><br>If the question says <i>organised</i>, think head office, not the shop floor.'},

 {t:'ot',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'A brand is fighting for shelf visibility in a neighbourhood kirana. What does visibility in GT depend on most?',
  opts:['The relationship with that individual retailer',
        'A display contract negotiated with the chain centrally',
        'Search ranking and sponsored listings on the platform',
        'The planogram that the retailer’s head office issues'],
  why:'<b>GT shelf visibility “depends heavily on retailer relationship”.</b> There is no head office and no planogram — the owner decides what goes at eye level, and the salesman’s relationship (plus schemes) is the lever.<div class="work">Shelf visibility\n  OT    → critical; negotiated shelf/display space\n  GT    → depends heavily on retailer relationship\n  E-com → digital visibility / search ranking</div>A centrally negotiated contract and a planogram are both OT answers dressed up. Search ranking is e-commerce.<br><br>In GT the relationship is the asset; in OT the contract is.'},

 {t:'ot',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'Where is inventory held in the <b>e-commerce</b> channel?',
  opts:['Warehouse / dark store / fulfilment centre',
        'Store plus the chain’s distribution centre',
        'Retailer’s backroom plus the distributor',
        'The manufacturer’s own factory depot only'],
  why:'<b>E-com inventory sits in warehouses, dark stores and fulfilment centres (FC).</b> Blinkit and Zepto run on dark stores; Amazon and Flipkart on FCs.<div class="work">Inventory\n  OT    → store + distribution centre / warehouse\n  GT    → retailer’s backroom + distributor\n  E-com → warehouse / dark store / FC</div>Each wrong option is the right answer for a different column — which is exactly how the paper will set it.<br><br>Learn the inventory row as a three-way set, not one answer at a time.'},

 {t:'ot',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which channel has pricing that is <b>“highly dynamic and promotion-driven”</b>?',
  opts:['E-commerce',
        'Organised Trade',
        'General Trade',
        'Cash & carry'],
  why:'<b>E-commerce pricing is “highly dynamic and promotion-driven”</b> — prices on Amazon or Blinkit can move several times a day with coupons and platform promotions.<div class="work">Pricing\n  OT    → more centrally controlled\n  GT    → retailer-dependent within market norms\n  E-com → highly dynamic and promotion-driven</div>OT is the opposite: <i>centrally controlled</i> prices across the chain. GT sits between — the retailer sets the price, but within what the market will bear.<br><br>(The slide prints “marker norms” — a typo for <i>market</i> norms.)'},

 {t:'ot',tag:'apply',diff:'easy',
  q:'A brand plan includes a <b>listing fee</b> and a negotiated gondola-end display. Which channel is this plan for?',
  opts:['Organised Trade',
        'General Trade',
        'E-commerce',
        'Any of them — these levers apply equally'],
  why:'<b>Listing fees and negotiated displays are OT promotion levers.</b><div class="work">Promotion\n  OT    → listing fees, trade promotions, displays,\n          visibility, category promotions\n  GT    → retailer schemes, discounts, displays,\n          salesman influence\n  E-com → search ranking, sponsored listings,\n          coupons, platform promotions</div>GT has displays too, but nobody charges a listing fee to get into a kirana — the owner just stocks what sells. E-com buys visibility through sponsored listings, not gondola ends.<br><br>“Listing fee” is the giveaway word: it only exists where a central buyer controls entry.'},

 {t:'ot',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'Who decides the assortment in a kirana store?',
  opts:['The retailer — GT assortment is retailer-specific',
        'The FMCG company’s category team, centrally',
        'A central buying team, store cluster by store cluster',
        'The distributor, through a fixed planogram it issues'],
  why:'<b>GT assortment is “retailer-specific”.</b> The kirana owner stocks what his customers ask for, fed by the distributor’s salesman. Nobody centrally plans it.<div class="work">Assortment\n  OT    → planned and centrally controlled\n  GT    → retailer-specific\n  E-com → very broad virtual assortment</div>“Store cluster” is the MT phrase from the supply-chain comparison — it is the trap. The distributor influences what is offered, but issues no planogram.<br><br>Centrally planned = OT. Retailer-specific = GT.'},

 {t:'shopper',tag:'apply',diff:'easy',
  q:'A shopper picks up a chocolate bar at the checkout counter she had no plan to buy. Which FMCG levers fit this shopper?',
  opts:['Secondary displays, checkout placement and attractive packaging',
        'Promotions, price-offs and multi-packs that trigger a switch at shelf',
        'Availability of preferred pack sizes and monthly value packs',
        'Cold cabinets, quick-commerce listing and on-the-go packs'],
  why:'<b>Impulse shopper → secondary displays, checkout placement, attractive packaging.</b> The purchase is triggered by visibility, appetite or curiosity, so the lever is being seen at the right moment.<div class="work">Planned       → availability, preferred pack sizes, value packs\nDeal-seeking  → promotions, price-offs, multi-packs\nImpulse       → secondary displays, checkout placement, packaging\nConvenience   → cold cabinets, quick commerce, on-the-go packs\nBrand-loyal   → availability, quality, consistent experience</div>A price-off is the deal-seeker’s lever — the impulse shopper did not compare prices at all.<br><br>Match the lever to what <i>triggered</i> the purchase.'},

 {t:'shopper',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Buying a ready-to-drink coffee during a commute is the classic example of which shopper type?',
  opts:['Convenience-seeking shopper',
        'Impulse shopper',
        'Brand-loyal (Nescafé) shopper',
        'Deal-seeking shopper'],
  why:'<b>Convenience-seeking: “prioritises speed, accessibility and ease of purchase.”</b> The RTD coffee on a commute is the classic example, and the levers are cold cabinets, quick-commerce availability and on-the-go packs.<div class="work">Convenience-seeking\n  Example → RTD coffee during a commute\n  Lever   → cold cabinets, quick commerce, on-the-go packs</div>It can look like impulse, but the coffee was a <i>need</i> met quickly, not an unplanned grab triggered by a display.<br><br>Impulse is triggered by the display; convenience is triggered by the shopper’s time pressure.'},

 {t:'shopper',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'A shopper always buys Nescafé Classic. What is the biggest commercial risk for Nestlé with this shopper?',
  opts:['A stock-out, which forces even a loyal shopper to switch',
        'Too few price-offs, since loyal shoppers chase discounts',
        'Weak checkout placement, which loses the impulse sale',
        'A missing multi-pack, which this shopper buys every time'],
  why:'<b>Brand-loyal shopper → “maintain availability, quality and consistent brand experience”.</b> Loyalty is not bought with discounts; it is kept by never giving the shopper a reason to try the alternative.<div class="work">Brand-loyal\n  Example → consistently buying Nescafé Classic\n  Lever   → availability · quality · consistent experience</div>The trap is assuming every shopper responds to price-offs. Discounting a loyal shopper’s brand mostly gives margin away to someone who would have bought anyway.<br><br>The loyal shopper’s enemy is the empty shelf, not the competitor’s offer.'},

 {t:'shopper',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'In the seven-step shopper journey, which stage comes straight after <b>“Navigates the store”</b>?',
  opts:['Category exploration',
        'Decision making',
        'Entry & first impressions',
        'Purchase & checkout'],
  why:'<b>The journey runs: Entry → Navigate → Category exploration → Decision → Purchase → Post-purchase → Repeat.</b><div class="work">1 Entry & first impressions\n2 Navigates the store\n3 Category exploration   ← compares brands, packs, prices\n4 Decision making\n5 Purchase & checkout\n6 Post-purchase behaviour\n7 Repeat visit</div>Decision making is one step later: first the shopper scans the category, <i>then</i> chooses.<br><br>A useful mnemonic: E-N-C-D-P-P-R — “Every New Customer Decides, Pays, Posts, Returns.”'},

 {t:'shopper',tag:'apply',diff:'hard',trap:true,
  q:'At which journey stage do eye-level placement and end caps act as <b>influencer cues</b>?',
  opts:['Decision making',
        'Category exploration',
        'Entry & first impressions',
        'Purchase & checkout'],
  why:'<b>Stage 4, Decision making:</b> “Chooses based on brand trust, price, quality, pack size, offers and influencer cues (e.g. eye-level, end caps).”<div class="work">3 Category exploration → compares brands, checks pack sizes, prices, offers\n4 Decision making      → trust, price, quality, pack, offers,\n                         influencer cues: eye-level, end caps</div>Category exploration is the tempting answer because the shopper is in front of the shelf — but the cues act at the moment of choice. Checkout is where <i>impulse</i> add-ons happen.<br><br>Exploration is comparing; decision is choosing — the cues tip the choice.'},

 {t:'shopper',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'A shopper picks up three shampoo brands, checks the variants and compares price points before choosing. Which shopper behaviour is this?',
  opts:['Explores & compares',
        'Value seeking',
        'Brand & quality conscious',
        'Convenience driven'],
  why:'<b>Explores & compares: “checks multiple brands, variants and price points.”</b><div class="work">Value seeking          → best price, offers, pack sizes\nConvenience driven     → easy-to-find, well-stocked products\nBrand & quality        → trusted brands, familiar packs\nExplores & compares    → multiple brands, variants, price points\nImpulse buying         → displays, end caps, combos, promotions\nHealth & lifestyle     → healthier, natural, functional</div>Value seeking also involves price, but it is about finding the <i>best</i> price or offer, not about comparing across brands and variants.<br><br>Read for the verb: “compares” across options vs “looks for” the best deal.'},

 {t:'layout',tag:'apply',diff:'easy',
  q:'A grocery retailer with a very high SKU count wants maximum product density and quick everyday shopping. Which layout fits?',
  opts:['Grid layout',
        'Free-flow layout',
        'Loop / racetrack layout',
        'Spine layout'],
  why:'<b>Grid: parallel aisles, straight pathways — “efficient, organised, high capacity”.</b> It maximises space and product density and suits everyday missions.<div class="work">Grid best suited for\n  grocery · pharmacies · convenience · high-SKU formats</div>Free-flow and loop give up density for experience — fine for fashion, wasteful for 5,000 grocery SKUs. Spine suits multi-department stores rather than dense grocery.<br><br>High SKUs + everyday mission = grid, every time.'},

 {t:'layout',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'A retailer wants shoppers <b>guided along one defined path</b> past every category, not wandering. Which layout is it?',
  opts:['Loop / racetrack layout',
        'Free-flow layout',
        'Spine layout with one main aisle',
        'Hybrid layout'],
  why:'<b>Loop / racetrack “creates a defined path that guides shoppers around the store, exposing them to multiple product categories”.</b><div class="work">Loop      → defined path, guided exposure, discovery\nFree-flow → flexible fixtures, no rigid aisles, open wandering</div>Free-flow is the trap: both are “experiential” and both suit fashion, but free-flow deliberately has <i>no</i> set path. The defined path is the loop’s whole point.<br><br>Loop = guided. Free-flow = unguided. That is the line examiners test.'},

 {t:'layout',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'A large electronics and home-appliances store (think Croma) wants a clear main aisle with departments branching off both sides. Which layout?',
  opts:['Spine layout',
        'Grid layout',
        'Loop / racetrack layout',
        'Free-flow layout'],
  why:'<b>Spine: “a central main aisle (spine) with product categories and departments branching off on either side.”</b> Clear path, strong structure.<div class="work">Spine best suited for\n  large-format stores · department stores\n  multi-category retailers · home improvement & electronics</div>A grid also has aisles, but they are many parallel ones of equal weight — there is no single spine.<br><br>One dominant aisle with departments off it = spine.'},

 {t:'layout',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'A hypermarket runs grocery aisles, a fashion zone and an electronics corner under one roof. Which layout fits best?',
  opts:['Hybrid layout',
        'Grid layout',
        'Spine layout',
        'Free-flow layout'],
  why:'<b>Hybrid “combines two or more layout types — a grid for core categories and free-flow or loop elements for featured areas.”</b><div class="work">Hybrid hypermarket floor\n  Everyday essentials → grid\n  Fashion             → free-flow\n  Electronics         → loop element\nBest suited for: superstores & hypermarkets, multiple shopping missions</div>Grid alone would serve the grocery and waste the fashion zone. Spine suits departments, but a multi-mission hypermarket is the textbook hybrid.<br><br>Several shopping missions in one store = hybrid.'},

 {t:'layout',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'What does a grid layout give up compared with a loop layout?',
  opts:['Browsing and discovery, since it is built for efficient missions',
        'Product density, since its aisles are spaced far apart from each other',
        'Ease of finding products, since its paths are curved',
        'Space efficiency, since it works only in small stores'],
  why:'<b>Grid optimises efficiency; loop optimises discovery.</b> The grid lets a shopper go straight to the aisle they need — great for a list, poor for exposing them to categories they did not plan to visit.<div class="work">Grid → efficient · organised · high capacity\nLoop → encourages discovery · engaging · experiential</div>The other options invert the grid’s strengths: it has the <i>highest</i> density, the <i>easiest</i> navigation and the <i>best</i> space use.<br><br>Every layout trades efficiency against experience — say which side it sits on.'},

 {t:'supply',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'In the MT supply chain, which step sits between <b>MT central buying</b> and the <b>MT store</b>?',
  opts:['MT DC / warehouse — receives, stores, sorts and distributes',
        'Distributor — stores stock and services retailers across a town',
        'Retailer / kirana — stocks and sells to end consumers',
        'Manufacturer depot — dispatches direct to each store'],
  why:'<b>MT chain: Manufacturer → MT central buying → MT DC/warehouse → MT store → shopper.</b><div class="work">MT : Manufacturer → Central Buying → DC/Warehouse → Store → Shopper\nGT : Manufacturer → Distributor → Retailer/Kirana → Shopper</div>The distributor is the GT intermediary. MT replaces it with its own DC, which is why “centralised buying, DC-based distribution” is the MT tagline.<br><br>MT has a buying step GT does not; GT has a distributor MT does not.'},

 {t:'supply',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'Which tagline describes the <b>General Trade</b> supply chain?',
  opts:['Distributor-led, decentralised and retailer-focused',
        'Centralised buying, DC-based, system-driven replenishment',
        'Platform-led, dark-store based and algorithm-driven',
        'Manufacturer-led, direct-to-store and forecast-driven'],
  why:'<b>GT: “distributor-led, decentralised and retailer-focused.”</b> <b>MT: “centralised buying, DC-based distribution and system-driven replenishment.”</b><div class="work">MT → centralised · DC-based · system-driven\nGT → distributor-led · decentralised · retailer-focused</div>The second option is the MT tagline — the most likely wrong pick. The third describes quick commerce, which the MT vs GT supply-chain comparison does not cover.<br><br>The two taglines are mirror images: learn one and invert it.'},

 {t:'supply',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'How is replenishment ordered for a DMart store?',
  opts:['System / central replenishment, driven by inventory and sales',
        'The store manager phones the FMCG company’s salesman every week',
        'The distributor’s salesman visits and writes the order',
        'The shopper’s app orders trigger a dark-store top-up'],
  why:'<b>MT ordering is “system / central replenishment”; the store “receives replenishment based on inventory/sales”.</b><div class="work">Ordering\n  MT → system / central replenishment\n  GT → salesman / distributor driven\nStore replenishment\n  MT → DC / system replenishes store\n  GT → distributor services outlet</div>The salesman-writes-the-order picture is GT. It is the trap because FMCG companies do still put people into MT stores — but they are promoters and merchandisers, not order-takers.<br><br>In MT the system orders; in GT a person does.'},

 {t:'supply',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Why can a brand measure promotion uplift far more precisely in MT than in GT?',
  opts:['MT has POS / sales data; GT data is often limited',
        'MT promotions run longer, so their effects are bigger',
        'GT promotions are illegal without a distributor',
        'MT shoppers are more responsive to every offer'],
  why:'<b>Data: MT → “POS / sales data available”; GT → “often limited”.</b> Store/SKU-level scan data lets the brand compare sales during and outside a promotion.<div class="work">Data\n  MT → POS / sales data available\n  GT → often limited</div>The same holds for national chains, which use “store/SKU-level sales, inventory, off-take and promotion data”. Measurability is the feature, not shopper responsiveness.<br><br>“System-led” in the GT/OT one-liner is largely this: decisions run on data.'},

 {t:'category',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'How is a <b>category</b> defined in MT?',
  opts:['A group of related products that meet a similar need',
        'All the SKUs of one brand on a single shelf in-store',
        'The products a retailer buys from a single supplier',
        'A price band that groups products of similar value'],
  why:'<b>“A category is a group of related products that meet a similar need”</b> — personal care, home care, beverages, snacks.<div class="work">Category = related products × similar need\nNOT      = one brand / one supplier / one price band</div>Defining by brand or supplier is the manufacturer’s view. MT is category-led precisely because the retailer organises around the shopper’s need, not the supplier.<br><br>In MT, “categories are the navigation points for shoppers”.'},

 {t:'category',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Dairy and personal care are described as categories that “act as destination points”. Which benefit of category does that illustrate?',
  opts:['Drives store traffic',
        'Builds larger baskets',
        'Increases sales and share',
        'Meets shopper needs better'],
  why:'<b>Benefit 1, Drives store traffic:</b> “Key categories act as destination points (e.g. dairy, personal care).” A shopper goes to the store <i>for</i> them.<div class="work">1 Drives store traffic        → destination categories\n2 Builds larger baskets       → cross-category, add-ons, higher bill\n3 Increases sales & share     → visibility, availability\n4 Meets shopper needs better  → navigation, experience\n5 Efficient space & assortment→ right SKUs, shelf, facings\n6 Long-term partnerships      → trust, promos, growth</div>Larger baskets is the next step — once the shopper is in for milk, cross-category buying adds to the bill.<br><br>Destination = traffic. Add-on = basket.'},

 {t:'category',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'A well-planned snacks aisle next to beverages leads shoppers to add chips to their cola. Which benefit is this?',
  opts:['Builds larger baskets',
        'Drives store traffic',
        'Enables efficient space management',
        'Supports long-term partnerships'],
  why:'<b>Builds larger baskets: “A well-planned category encourages cross-category buying … drives add-on purchases and higher average bill value.”</b><div class="work">Cola (planned) + chips (add-on) → higher average bill value</div>Traffic is about why the shopper came in; basket is about what else they leave with. Space management is about allocating SKUs, shelf and facings.<br><br>Cross-category = basket.'},

 {t:'category',tag:'calc',diff:'hard',trap:true,
  q:'Fresh foods is ranked <b>7th</b> at 10–20%; personal care is ranked <b>2nd</b> at 10–15%. What should you take from this?',
  opts:['The ranks do not follow the ranges — fresh’s midpoint, 15%, beats personal care’s 12.5%',
        'Personal care always out-sells fresh, which is why it ranks 2nd in the table',
        'Fresh foods’ range is a misprint and should read 1–2%, in line with its rank',
        'Ranks are by margin, not by sales, so the ranges and the ranks cannot be compared at all'],
  why:'<b>The table’s ranks and ranges disagree.</b> Compare midpoints:<div class="work">Personal care (rank 2)  (10 + 15) ÷ 2 = 12.5%\nFresh foods  (rank 7)  (10 + 20) ÷ 2 = 15.0%\nDairy        (rank 6)  ( 8 + 15) ÷ 2 = 11.5%\nHome care    (rank 3)  ( 8 + 12) ÷ 2 = 10.0%</div>On the table’s own numbers, fresh can be the second-largest category and dairy outranks home care. The ranges also sum to 82–139%, so they are indicative, not a split of 100.<br><br><b>In the exam:</b> quote the ranges, call them “indicative”, and do not build an argument on the rank order below #1. Nothing on the slide says the ranks are by margin — that option invents a reason.'},

 {t:'category',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which benefit of category in MT is about allocating the right SKUs, shelf space and facings?',
  opts:['Enables efficient space & assortment management',
        'Meets shopper needs better',
        'Increases sales & share',
        'Supports long-term partnerships with the retailers'],
  why:'<b>Benefit 5: “Helps allocate the right SKUs, shelf space and facings; improves productivity of the store and the sales team.”</b><div class="work">5 Efficient space & assortment → SKUs · shelf space · facings · productivity</div>“Increases sales & share” is about visibility and availability for the <i>brand</i>; benefit 5 is about the retailer’s <i>space</i> productivity.<br><br>Facings and shelf space → space management.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'A chain with <b>40 stores</b> wants to list your brand. Who decides and how do you work with them?',
  opts:['Central buyer + regional teams; regional negotiation, centralised listing',
        'Store owner; a direct store-level relationship, store by store',
        'Owner / small buying team; direct negotiation, store-wise execution',
        'Central category team; national agreements and a centralised promotions calendar'],
  why:'<b>10–100 stores = regional chain: central buyer + regional teams; regional negotiations, centralised listing.</b><div class="work">Construct            Stores    Decision maker              FMCG approach\nSingle store         1         Store owner                 Direct store-level relationship\nSmall chain          2–10      Owner / small buying team   Direct negotiation + store-wise execution\nRegional chain       10–100    Central buyer + regional    Regional negotiations, centralised listing\nLarge regional/nat.  100–500   Central category/procurement Central listing + region/store execution\nNational / pan-India 500+      Central buying + cat. mgmt  National agreements, centralised promotions</div>40 stores is past the owner-run stage but well short of national.<br><br>Read the store count, then walk the row: decision maker → approach.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'What is the typical store-area range for a <b>national hypermarket chain</b> such as DMart or Reliance Smart Bazaar?',
  opts:['30,000–1,00,000+ sq ft',
        '2,000–15,000 sq ft',
        '1,000–5,000 sq ft',
        '50,000–2,00,000+ sq ft'],
  why:'<b>National hypermarkets: 30,000–1,00,000+ sq ft</b> (DMart, Reliance Smart Bazaar, Spencer’s).<div class="work">Hypermarket chains  30,000–1,00,000+  DMart · Smart Bazaar · Spencer’s\nSupermarket chains   2,000–15,000     Nature’s Basket · More · Star Bazaar\nCash & carry        30,000–1,00,000+  METRO\nLulu (regional)     50,000–2,00,000+\nNilgiris             1,000–5,000</div>2,000–15,000 is the supermarket band; 50,000–2,00,000+ is Lulu specifically.<br><br>Hypermarket and cash & carry share a size band — what separates them is the customer (shopper vs business).'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'National MT chains work with FMCG companies through which relationship model?',
  opts:['Joint Business Planning (JBP), not transactional selling',
        'Transactional selling, order by order through the whole year',
        'Owner-led negotiation with quick, informal decisions',
        'Distributor-led servicing with local credit terms'],
  why:'<b>Relationship model: “Joint Business Planning (JBP) rather than transactional selling.”</b> Brand and retailer set joint growth targets, promotion calendars and investment for the year.<div class="work">National chain ways of working\n  Buying      → centralised national/category teams\n  Negotiation → annual / periodic, national or regional\n  Promotions  → annual promotion calendar\n  Review      → regular business reviews: sales, growth, availability, ROI\n  Relationship→ JBP rather than transactional selling</div>Owner-led, quick decisions is how <i>regional</i> chains work. Distributor-led servicing is GT.<br><br>Scale makes the relationship a plan, not a series of orders.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'apply',diff:'hard',trap:true,
  q:'Lulu Hypermarket runs 50,000–2,00,000+ sq ft stores, yet it is classed as a <b>regional</b> MT chain. How do you reconcile this?',
  opts:['Construct (reach) and format (store size) are separate axes',
        'It is an error — any hypermarket must be a national chain',
        'Store size decides the construct, so Lulu is national',
        'Regional chains are defined by store size under 15,000 sq ft'],
  why:'<b>Construct and format answer different questions.</b> Construct (single, regional, national) is about <i>reach and who decides</i>. Format (hyper, super, mini, cash & carry) is about <i>store size and offer</i>.<div class="work">Lulu       → format: hypermarket · construct: regional (selected cities)\nNilgiris   → format: supermarket · construct: regional (South India)\nDMart      → format: hypermarket · construct: national</div>The trap is assuming big stores mean a national chain. A regional chain can run hypermarkets; a national chain can run 1,000 mini stores.<br><br>Always classify a retailer on both axes.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'How does a <b>regional</b> MT chain differ from a <b>national</b> one?',
  opts:['More scope for regional assortment and local festival campaigns',
        'More standardised core assortment and national promotion calendars',
        'More formal, structured business reviews across every chain',
        'More large-scale volume and reach than a national chain has'],
  why:'<b>Regional chains: regional/owner-led buying, regional assortment, local festivals, reviews that vary by chain capability, strong local presence.</b><div class="work">National                      Regional\nNational/category buying      Regional buyers / owner-led\nStandardised core assortment  Greater scope for regional assortment\nNational promo calendars      Regional festivals, local campaigns\nFormal structured reviews     Reviews vary by size/capability\nLarge-scale volume and reach  Strong local presence, shopper insight</div>The other three options are all national-chain traits, reworded.<br><br>Regional wins on local relevance and speed; national wins on scale and structure.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Why does a cash & carry (e.g. METRO) price differently from a hypermarket?',
  opts:['Net prices must leave room for downstream retailers to earn a margin',
        'It sells to end shoppers, so it matches the lowest MRP in its catchment area',
        'It runs on JBPs, so the FMCG company sets the shelf price itself',
        'It stocks premium imported brands, so it prices at a premium'],
  why:'<b>Cash & carry sells B2B</b> — to kiranas, food service and other businesses. Its pricing must be “competitive net purchase prices that allow downstream retailers to earn a margin”.<div class="work">Cash & carry\n  Customers   → kiranas, food service, other B2B\n  Assortment  → high-rotation SKUs, bulk packs, case sizes\n  Promotions  → volume discounts, slab schemes, bulk deals\n  Supply      → pallet/carton movement, high fill rates\n  Metrics     → volume/value, gross margin, stock turns,\n                availability, customer penetration</div>The second option describes a consumer retailer. Cash & carry’s customer resells the product, so it needs headroom to MRP.<br><br>In cash & carry the shopper is a business — every row follows from that.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which set of levers best fits a <b>mini supermarket</b> chain (e.g. Reliance Smart Point)?',
  opts:['Small packs, impulse lines, checkout displays and chillers',
        'Bulk packs, case sizes and volume slab schemes for business',
        'Premium imported brands, testers and beauty advisors on-floor',
        'Annual national calendars and a JBP with category management'],
  why:'<b>Mini supermarkets: limited shelf space, frequent small-basket shopping.</b><div class="work">Assortment  → small packs, impulse, daily essentials, beverages, snacks\nVisibility  → checkout displays, counter-top units, shelf strips,\n              eye-level placement, chillers\nSupply      → frequent replenishment in small quantities\nMetrics     → sales per sq ft, SKU productivity, stock-outs,\n              stock turns, repeat purchase</div>Bulk packs are cash & carry; testers and advisors are beauty retail; JBP and national calendars are the national hypermarket.<br><br>Small store, small basket, small pack — and sales per square foot is the metric.'},

 {t:'construct',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'Which type of MT chain can have the <b>widest</b> store network?',
  opts:['Convenience / mini-supermarket chains, at 10–1,000+ stores',
        'National / large hypermarket chains, at 100+ stores',
        'Supermarket chains such as More, at 20–500+ stores in their network',
        'Cash & carry / wholesale chains, at 20–100+ stores'],
  why:'<b>Convenience / mini-supermarket chains: 10–1,000+ stores</b> (Reliance Smart Point, Spencer’s Local). Small stores, many of them.<div class="work">National / large hypermarket   100+\nSupermarket chains             20–500\nRegional MT chains             10–100+\nCash & carry / wholesale       20–100+\nConvenience / mini-supermarket 10–1,000+</div>The trap is equating big stores with a big network. Hypermarkets are large but expensive to open; mini formats scale by number.<br><br>Note: the store-count construct table puts a national chain at 500+ stores, while the format summary puts national hypermarkets at 100+. Quote the table the question refers to.'},

 {t:'specialty',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'What is the typical assortment of a pharmacy retail chain?',
  opts:['Medicines + OTC + personal care + wellness + nutrition + selected FMCG',
        'Prescription medicines only, with no FMCG or personal-care range at all',
        'Makeup, skincare, haircare and fragrance with premium international labels',
        'High-rotation bulk packs and case sizes for clinics and hospital buyers'],
  why:'<b>Pharmacy assortment: medicines + OTC + personal care + wellness + nutrition + selected FMCG.</b> That is exactly why pharmacy is an MT channel for FMCG companies.<div class="work">FMCG opportunity in pharmacy\n  OTC · personal care · oral care · skincare · baby care\n  nutrition · hygiene · wellness</div>“Medicines only” describes an old-style chemist. The makeup list is the beauty retailer’s assortment.<br><br>Pharmacy = health trust + FMCG adjacency.'},

 {t:'specialty',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'What data does a pharmacy chain track that a grocery chain would not?',
  opts:['The prescription / OTC mix',
        'Promotion uplift store by store',
        'SKU sales and inventory',
        'Store-level productivity'],
  why:'<b>Pharmacy data: “SKU sales, inventory, prescription/OTC mix, customer purchase and store productivity.”</b> The Rx/OTC mix is unique to the channel.<div class="work">Pharmacy data → SKU sales · inventory · prescription/OTC mix\n                customer purchase · store productivity</div>The other three are tracked by every organised chain.<br><br>For an FMCG brand, a high OTC share signals a store where self-selected health products sell.'},

 {t:'specialty',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which chain is India’s <b>second-largest</b> pharmacy retailer, with 5,500+ stores in 800+ cities?',
  opts:['MedPlus',
        'Apollo Pharmacy',
        'Guardian Pharmacy',
        'Wellness Forever'],
  why:'<b>MedPlus Health Services: second-largest, 5,500+ stores, 800+ cities</b> — selling medicines, FMCG and opticals.<div class="work">Largest       → Apollo Pharmacy (Apollo Hospitals Enterprise)\nSecond-largest→ MedPlus (5,500+ stores, 800+ cities)</div>Apollo is the largest. Guardian and Wellness Forever are smaller, regionally concentrated chains.<br><br>Learn Apollo and MedPlus as a pair.'},

 {t:'specialty',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which visibility levers belong to a beauty retailer, as opposed to a grocery chain?',
  opts:['Brand bays, shop-in-shops, testers and digital screens',
        'End caps, gondola ends and secondary displays in aisles',
        'Checkout displays, counter-top units and shelf strips',
        'Pallet displays and case stacks in a warehouse aisle'],
  why:'<b>Beauty visibility: brand bays, gondolas, shop-in-shops, testers and digital screens.</b> Beauty is bought on trial, so the visibility is built around trying the product.<div class="work">Beauty visibility → brand bays · gondolas · shop-in-shops · testers · screens\nBeauty experience → trials · beauty advisors · consultations · demos</div>End caps are the hypermarket set; checkout units are the mini-supermarket set; pallets are cash & carry.<br><br>In beauty the tester does the job the end cap does in grocery.'},

 {t:'specialty',tag:'concept',diff:'easy',
  q:'How is <b>Sephora</b> positioned among India’s beauty retailers?',
  opts:['Premium / prestige beauty',
        'Multi-brand specialist + omnichannel',
        'Beauty, personal care and wellness',
        'Premium beauty + omnichannel'],
  why:'<b>Sephora = premium / prestige beauty.</b><div class="work">Nykaa         → multi-brand beauty specialist + omnichannel\nSephora       → premium / prestige beauty\nTira          → premium beauty + omnichannel\nHealth & Glow → beauty, personal care and wellness\nNewU          → beauty, personal care and wellness</div>Tira is the near-miss: premium too, but positioned as omnichannel (Reliance’s app + stores).<br><br>Prestige is Sephora’s word.'},

 {t:'specialty',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which is the defining <b>assortment strategy</b> for beauty retail?',
  opts:['Premiumisation — premium, international, indie and emerging brands',
        'Value packs and price-offs on everyday mass-market personal-care brands',
        'Bulk packs and case sizes to serve salons and spa businesses',
        'Prescription-led ranges with OTC skincare as an add-on line'],
  why:'<b>Beauty retail has a dedicated “premiumization” row: a strong focus on premium, international, indie and emerging brands.</b><div class="work">Beauty: curated portfolio across makeup, skincare, haircare,\n        fragrance, personal care\n        + premiumisation (premium · international · indie · emerging)\n        + brand partnership (joint launches, exclusives, activations)</div>Value packs are the grocery deal-seeker lever, not the beauty strategy.<br><br>Beauty MT is where indie and D2C brands go for premium visibility.'},

 {t:'org',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'Which ordering matches the Modern Trade & E-com sales hierarchy, top to bottom?',
  opts:['Sales VP → KAM → RSM MT → ASM MT → Territory In-Charge → Promoter',
        'Sales VP → RSM MT → KAM → ASM MT → Promoter → Territory In-Charge',
        'KAM → Sales VP → RSM MT → Territory In-Charge → ASM MT → Promoter',
        'Sales VP → ASM MT → RSM MT → KAM → Territory In-Charge → Promoter'],
  why:'<b>Sales VP → Key Account Manager → RSM MT (North/East/West/South) → ASM MT → Territory In-Charge → Promoter.</b><div class="work">Sales VP\n └ Key Account Manager\n    └ RSM MT  North · East · West · South\n       └ ASM MT\n          └ Territory In-Charge\n             └ Promoter (in-store)</div>Region (RSM) sits above area (ASM), which sits above territory. The KAM sits above the regions because MT accounts are negotiated centrally.<br><br>Region → area → territory → store: largest to smallest.'},

 {t:'org',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'Why does the Key Account Manager sit <b>above</b> the regional sales managers in the MT hierarchy?',
  opts:['MT accounts are bought centrally, so the account outranks the region',
        'KAMs manage more promoters than the RSMs do, so they rank as more senior',
        'KAMs handle GT distributors, who drive more volume than MT',
        'RSMs only manage e-commerce platforms, a smaller channel'],
  why:'<b>MT buying is centralised, so the commercial relationship is with the account, not the region.</b> One DMart negotiation covers every DMart region; the RSMs then execute it regionally.<div class="work">Who decides on the retailer side  → central buyer / category manager\nWho faces them on the FMCG side   → Key Account Manager\nWho executes regionally           → RSM → ASM → TI → Promoter</div>The trap is reading seniority as headcount. GT distributors are not a KAM’s job, and RSMs here are “RSM MT”, not e-commerce.<br><br>Structure follows the buyer: central buyer, central KAM.'},

 {t:'org',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'In a retailer’s category-team hierarchy, which role sits at the same level as the Category Manager?',
  opts:['Merchandising Manager',
        'Category Head',
        'Category Executive',
        'Head of Buying & Merchandising'],
  why:'<b>Head of Buying & Merchandising → Category Head → Category Manager + Merchandising Manager → Category Executive</b>, supported by supply chain, marketing and store operations.<div class="work">Head of Buying & Merchandising\n  └ Category Head\n     ├ Category Manager   ─┐ same level\n     └ Merchandising Mgr  ─┘\n        └ Category Executive\n(Support: Supply chain | Marketing | Store Operations)</div>The category manager buys and plans the range; the merchandising manager runs how it is presented.<br><br>Buy (category) and present (merchandising) sit side by side.'},

 {t:'org',tag:'concept',diff:'medium',
  q:'What is the correct order of the five steps for how the category team works with FMCG companies?',
  opts:['Planning → negotiation → range & shelf → promotion & execution → review',
        'Negotiation → planning → promotion & execution → range & shelf → review',
        'Planning → range & shelf → negotiation → review → promotion & execution',
        'Range & shelf → planning → negotiation → promotion & execution → review'],
  why:'<b>1 Category planning → 2 Supplier negotiation → 3 Range & shelf planning → 4 Promotion & execution → 5 Performance review.</b><div class="work">1 Category planning   → growth opportunities, shopper needs, target sales/margins\n2 Supplier negotiation→ listing, assortment, trade terms, pricing, promo investment\n3 Range & shelf       → SKUs, shelf space, planograms, store assortments\n4 Promotion & exec.   → campaigns, visibility, availability, store implementation\n5 Performance review  → sales, gross margin, stock turns, availability,\n                        market share, promotion ROI</div>The logic: decide what the category needs, agree terms, then lay out the shelf, then execute, then measure.<br><br>Plan → buy → place → push → prove.'},

 {t:'org',tag:'apply',diff:'medium',trap:true,
  q:'In which step of the category team’s process are <b>planograms</b> defined?',
  opts:['Range & shelf planning',
        'Promotion & execution in-store',
        'Category planning',
        'Supplier negotiation'],
  why:'<b>Range & shelf planning: “select SKUs, define shelf space, planograms and store-specific assortments.”</b><div class="work">3 Range & shelf planning → SKUs · shelf space · planograms · store assortments\n4 Promotion & execution  → campaigns · visibility · availability · store implementation</div>Promotion & execution is the trap — it covers <i>implementing</i> the plan in-store, but the planogram is designed in step 3.<br><br>Design in step 3; deliver in step 4.'}
];

